  "slug": "aggregation-amyloid-ed81",
  "summary": "Residue-resolved MD now maps aggregation-prone contacts in Tau, α-synuclein, Aβ and myocilin, linking modulators to specific structural determinants.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is simulation-based localization of aggregation-determining interfaces at residue resolution. The spermine preprint applies all-atom MD to Tau K18, α-synuclein and Aβ40 and finds residue-specific rather than net-charge-driven control of fibril-like contacts. The KLVFF study simulates an Aβ hexamer, where fibril-end binding destabilizes terminal monomers, and CD and ThT assays support inhibition. The myocilin study combines 21 μs of MD with GaMD to map five aggregation-prone regions in the olfactomedin domain linked to blade A destabilization. What is new is explicit assignment of determinants to specific residues and regions, with modulator and inhibitor effects evaluated against structural contacts. The myocilin case extends this logic beyond canonical amyloid proteins.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-12-30"
}
